 Byte-level language models are motivated by their ability to operate directly on raw bytes without relying on subword tokenization. To assess whether this advantage is preserved in practice, we evaluate the models along two complementary dimensions: general performance and robustness to input perturbations.
 
 \textbf{General Performance.}
-We assess general modeling capability using bits per byte (BPB), standard downstream accuracy, inference latency, and retrieval accuracy. BPB is measured on a fixed held-out DCLM validation split ($320$ non-overlapping $3{,}840$-byte windows), normalizing every model's log-likelihood by the same byte count; Appendix~\ref{app:bpb} details the computation and additionally reports the training-EMA readout at the iso-data endpoint, which preserves the same model ordering. For downstream evaluation, we report mean zero-, three-, and five-shot accuracy across HellaSwag~\cite{zellers2019hellaswag}, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge~\cite{arc}, PIQA~\cite{PIQA}, and WinoGrande~\cite{ai2:winogrande}.\footnote{BoolQ~\cite{clark2019boolq} and MMLU~\cite{mmlu} are also evaluated, but excluded here because some few-shot examples exceed the models' context length.} \hyun{We additionally report zero-shot LAMBADA~\cite{lambada}, which asks for the last word of a passage; an item counts as correct only if the model's greedy prediction reproduces the whole target word, so subword and byte-level models are scored on the identical string (Appendix~\ref{app:lambada}).} We also evaluate retrieval accuracy using \textit{Needle-in-a-Haystack} (NIAH) tasks~\cite{kamradt2023needle}. Following the protocols of~\citet{hsieh2024ruler} while aligning the context size of the trained models, we report averaged accuracy on Single NIAH (S-NIAH-1/2/3). 
